1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,370
Listen. Practice. Speak. Practice makes you perfect.

2
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:18,330
Good morning. Thanks for joining us today. Let's begin with a simple question.

3
00:00:18,330 --> 00:00:24,330
Could you tell me a little about your background and what inspired you to move into brand management?

4
00:00:24,330 --> 00:00:26,330
Good morning. Thank you for having me.

5
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:33,330
I started my career in marketing research, which gave me a strong foundation in understanding consumer behavior.

6
00:00:33,330 --> 00:00:39,530
Over time, I became fascinated with how brands shape decisions and build trust.

7
00:00:39,530 --> 00:00:44,330
That curiosity evolved into a passion for brand building, and that's what led me into

8
00:00:44,330 --> 00:00:45,970
brand management.

9
00:00:45,970 --> 00:00:47,290
Interesting.

10
00:00:47,290 --> 00:00:51,730
When you think about your role as a brand manager, what would you say is the central

11
00:00:51,730 --> 00:00:53,330
responsibility?

12
00:00:53,330 --> 00:00:58,710
I believe the central responsibility is to protect and grow the brand's equity.

13
00:00:58,710 --> 00:01:04,710
That includes everything from ensuring consistent communication to understanding market trends,

14
00:01:04,710 --> 00:01:08,390
maintaining product relevance, and driving long-term growth.

15
00:01:08,390 --> 00:01:12,950
A brand manager must balance creative thinking with commercial discipline.

16
00:01:12,950 --> 00:01:13,950
Excellent.

17
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:17,290
Now, I'd like you to imagine something.

18
00:01:17,290 --> 00:01:22,510
Suppose you inherit a brand with strong awareness but declining penetration.

19
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:23,990
What would be your approach?

20
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:29,410
First, I'd identify what's stopping consumers from converting awareness to purchase.

21
00:01:29,410 --> 00:01:34,950
I would analyze pricing, pack sizes, communication gaps, and competitive activity.

22
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:39,350
I might also conduct shopper research to understand and store barriers.

23
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:44,430
Once the insight is clear, I'd craft targeted interventions, whether that means a trial

24
00:01:44,430 --> 00:01:50,030
campaign, bundle packs, packaging refresh, or better visibility at retail.

25
00:01:50,030 --> 00:01:51,030
Good.

26
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:53,070
Let's look at competition.

27
00:01:53,070 --> 00:01:58,710
Imagine your main competitor suddenly launches an aggressive campaign with heavy discounts

28
00:01:58,710 --> 00:02:01,070
and strong digital presence.

29
00:02:01,070 --> 00:02:02,550
How do you respond?

30
00:02:02,550 --> 00:02:05,150
I wouldn't panic or copy them immediately.

31
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:10,350
First, I'd analyze whether the campaign is creating long-term value or just short-term

32
00:02:10,350 --> 00:02:11,510
noise.

33
00:02:11,510 --> 00:02:16,150
If their message threatens our positioning, I'd strengthen our communication and highlight

34
00:02:16,150 --> 00:02:18,270
our core differentiators.

35
00:02:18,270 --> 00:02:23,730
If it's purely a price war, I would consider selective tactical offers without compromising

36
00:02:23,730 --> 00:02:25,450
our brand premium.

37
00:02:25,450 --> 00:02:28,850
The key is to stay strategic, not reactive.

38
00:02:28,850 --> 00:02:29,850
Very good.

39
00:02:29,850 --> 00:02:32,010
Let's talk consumer insights.

40
00:02:32,010 --> 00:02:36,930
How do you ensure your strategic decisions are genuinely driven by insight and not

41
00:02:36,930 --> 00:02:38,530
just assumptions?

42
00:02:38,530 --> 00:02:42,290
I believe in triangulation using multiple sources.

43
00:02:42,290 --> 00:02:47,970
I review quantitative data, conduct qualitative interviews, observe shopper behavior and

44
00:02:47,970 --> 00:02:54,050
monitor digital conversations. Insights emerge when these sources point toward the same tension

45
00:02:54,050 --> 00:02:58,690
or unmet need. It's about interpreting patterns, not just collecting information.

46
00:02:59,330 --> 00:03:05,410
Good answer. Now let's shift to product development. What role do you see a brand

47
00:03:05,410 --> 00:03:10,770
manager playing in the innovation process? A brand manager acts as a bridge between

48
00:03:10,770 --> 00:03:16,050
consumer needs and technical feasibility. I help identify opportunity spaces,

49
00:03:16,050 --> 00:03:20,690
define the product concept and ensure the value proposition is clear.

50
00:03:20,690 --> 00:03:25,890
Throughout the process, I collaborate closely with R&D, supply chain and finance

51
00:03:25,890 --> 00:03:29,090
to make sure the idea is both desirable and viable.

52
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:33,010
My role is to keep the project aligned with the brand strategy.

53
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:36,210
Let's imagine an internal challenge.

54
00:03:36,210 --> 00:03:41,970
Suppose your sales team pushes for a low priced variant to compete with an economy brand,

55
00:03:41,970 --> 00:03:45,410
but you worry it will dilute your premium positioning.

56
00:03:45,410 --> 00:03:47,490
How do you handle the conflict?

57
00:03:47,490 --> 00:03:49,650
I'd start by understanding their reasoning.

58
00:03:49,650 --> 00:03:54,930
Usually it's driven by pressure from retailers or declining share in certain channels.

59
00:03:54,930 --> 00:04:00,290
I would then explain the long-term risks of brand dilution, showing data from similar cases.

60
00:04:00,930 --> 00:04:05,970
Instead of rejecting the idea outright, I might propose a controlled pilot test

61
00:04:05,970 --> 00:04:10,370
or suggest alternative PAC formats instead of lowering the overall brand price.

62
00:04:10,930 --> 00:04:14,530
Collaboration is essential, but so is protecting brand equity.

63
00:04:14,530 --> 00:04:15,530
Good.

64
00:04:15,530 --> 00:04:17,410
Good morning.

65
00:04:17,410 --> 00:04:19,370
Thank you for joining us today.

66
00:04:19,370 --> 00:04:21,410
Let's start with something simple.

67
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:26,470
Could you briefly introduce yourself and explain how your career has evolved in the direction

68
00:04:26,470 --> 00:04:28,290
of brand management?

69
00:04:28,290 --> 00:04:31,170
Good morning, and thank you for the opportunity.

70
00:04:31,170 --> 00:04:38,130
I started in general marketing, coordinating campaigns, promotions, and digital activities.

71
00:04:38,130 --> 00:04:44,210
Over time, I found myself drawn to the bigger picture thinking behind brand development,

72
00:04:44,210 --> 00:04:51,770
understanding consumer psychology, shaping perceptions, and building long-term brand value.

73
00:04:51,770 --> 00:04:58,390
That interest led me into brand roles where I've helped manage portfolios, launch innovations,

74
00:04:58,390 --> 00:05:04,030
run large campaigns, and collaborate across functions to deliver growth.

75
00:05:04,030 --> 00:05:05,030
Thank you.

76
00:05:05,030 --> 00:05:11,050
Now when you're given a brand, new or existing, what's the first thing you do to understand

77
00:05:11,050 --> 00:05:12,650
its current health?

78
00:05:12,650 --> 00:05:17,410
I begin by analyzing how consumers perceive the brand today.

79
00:05:17,410 --> 00:05:24,970
I look at brand equity scores, awareness levels, consideration, and repeat purchase behavior.

80
00:05:24,970 --> 00:05:31,010
Then I review performance data such as market share, distribution, pricing, and competitor

81
00:05:31,010 --> 00:05:32,290
activity.

82
00:05:32,290 --> 00:05:39,330
I combine that with qualitative inputs from consumers, retailers, and digital sentiment.

83
00:05:39,330 --> 00:05:45,370
This gives me a clear picture of where the brand stands and what gaps need to be addressed.

84
00:05:45,370 --> 00:05:50,850
And when you identify a gap, how do you decide whether it requires communication, product

85
00:05:50,850 --> 00:05:53,170
improvement, or something else?

86
00:05:53,170 --> 00:05:58,690
I break it down into what I call the why, what, how framework.

87
00:05:58,690 --> 00:06:00,550
Why is the gap happening?

88
00:06:00,550 --> 00:06:03,690
What exactly is affecting consumer decisions?

89
00:06:03,690 --> 00:06:06,330
How can the brand address it?

90
00:06:06,330 --> 00:06:13,010
Once the issue is functional, taste, packaging, format, other times it's emotional, lack

91
00:06:13,010 --> 00:06:16,370
of connection, outdated positioning.

92
00:06:16,370 --> 00:06:22,170
Once I understand the root cause, the solution becomes clear, whether it's product renovation,

93
00:06:22,170 --> 00:06:27,130
a packaging upgrade, or a refreshed communication platform.

94
00:06:27,130 --> 00:06:29,170
Let's talk execution.

95
00:06:29,170 --> 00:06:31,730
Suppose you're planning a major campaign.

96
00:06:31,730 --> 00:06:35,130
Walk me through how you manage that process.

97
00:06:35,130 --> 00:06:42,310
First I define the campaign objective, awareness, trial, consideration, or frequency.

98
00:06:42,310 --> 00:06:47,210
Then I gather insight to understand what emotional hook will resonate.

99
00:06:47,210 --> 00:06:52,890
I work with the agency to develop a strong idea that can translate across channels.

100
00:06:52,890 --> 00:06:58,790
I ensure the messaging ladder is clear, the visual identity is aligned with the brand,

101
00:06:58,790 --> 00:07:02,670
and the media mix is optimized for the target audience.

102
00:07:02,670 --> 00:07:09,870
Using execution, I monitor performance weekly, digital engagement, sales uplift, and feedback

103
00:07:09,870 --> 00:07:11,270
from the field.

104
00:07:11,270 --> 00:07:17,230
And finally, I evaluate the campaign's impact on the brand and document learnings for future

105
00:07:17,230 --> 00:07:18,230
planning.

106
00:07:18,230 --> 00:07:23,590
Can you share an example of a campaign that performed exceptionally well under your leadership?

107
00:07:23,590 --> 00:07:24,590
Yes.

108
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:28,670
We were promoting a product in a highly competitive category.

109
00:07:28,670 --> 00:07:34,510
Instead of traditional ads, we built the campaign around real consumer stories.

110
00:07:34,510 --> 00:07:41,310
We worked with micro-influencers, created short-form content, and partnered with retailers for sampling.

111
00:07:41,310 --> 00:07:48,650
The authentic storytelling angle created strong emotional appeal, engagement exceeded expectations,

112
00:07:48,650 --> 00:07:53,550
and the campaign delivered a significant uplift in trial and penetration.

113
00:07:53,550 --> 00:08:00,230
It reminded me that consumers respond strongly when communication feels relatable and human.

114
00:08:00,230 --> 00:08:01,470
Interesting.

115
00:08:01,470 --> 00:08:06,910
How do you manage innovation projects, especially when multiple stakeholders are involved?

116
00:08:06,910 --> 00:08:10,830
For innovation to succeed, alignment is critical.

117
00:08:10,830 --> 00:08:17,470
I start by building a solid case, consumer insight, market gap, feasibility, and business

118
00:08:17,470 --> 00:08:19,070
potential.

119
00:08:19,070 --> 00:08:25,670
Then I work closely with R&D to test formulations, with procurement to evaluate cost structures,

120
00:08:25,670 --> 00:08:29,590
and with supply chain to confirm production capability.

121
00:08:29,590 --> 00:08:35,030
Throughout the project, I keep everyone updated with clear timelines and expectations.

122
00:08:35,030 --> 00:08:41,710
I've learned that innovation fails not because of lack of ideas, but because of poor coordination.

123
00:08:41,710 --> 00:08:48,230
I make sure everyone feels part of the process and understands the purpose behind the innovation.

124
00:08:48,230 --> 00:08:54,030
shift to numbers. How comfortable are you with financial responsibilities like P&L or

125
00:08:54,030 --> 00:09:00,230
pricing decisions? Very comfortable. A brand manager is not just a creative role. It's

126
00:09:00,230 --> 00:09:06,870
also a commercial one. I work with finance to understand margins, cogs, tradespend, and

127
00:09:06,870 --> 00:09:13,750
ROI. When pricing changes are needed, I evaluate elasticity. Good morning. Thank

128
00:09:13,750 --> 00:09:15,650
Thank you for joining us today.

129
00:09:15,650 --> 00:09:20,830
To start off, could you tell me a bit about your professional background and what sparked

130
00:09:20,830 --> 00:09:23,630
your interest in brand management?

131
00:09:23,630 --> 00:09:25,990
Good morning and thank you for inviting me.

132
00:09:25,990 --> 00:09:31,030
I began my career in trade marketing, where I handled activation programs and in-store

133
00:09:31,030 --> 00:09:32,430
visibility.

134
00:09:32,430 --> 00:09:36,990
That experience taught me how consumers interact with brands at the point of purchase.

135
00:09:36,990 --> 00:09:41,710
Later, I moved into digital communication, and that's where I really understood how

136
00:09:41,710 --> 00:09:47,070
storytelling influences brand perception. Over time, I realized I wanted to be more

137
00:09:47,070 --> 00:09:52,910
involved in shaping strategy, not just execution. That's what drew me toward brand management.

138
00:09:53,710 --> 00:10:00,590
Great. In your view, what differentiates an average brand from a truly powerful brand?

139
00:10:01,310 --> 00:10:07,310
A powerful brand has a purpose, a personality, and a promise that stays consistent across

140
00:10:07,310 --> 00:10:13,230
every touch point. It knows who its customers are, what it stands for, and how to communicate

141
00:10:13,230 --> 00:10:18,670
in a memorable way. An average brand just reacts. A powerful brand leads.

142
00:10:19,390 --> 00:10:27,070
Nicely put, let's imagine a scenario. Your brand is suffering from stagnation. There's no decline,

143
00:10:27,070 --> 00:10:31,150
but no meaningful growth either. How would you approach this?

144
00:10:31,150 --> 00:10:35,350
I would start by identifying what's limiting growth.

145
00:10:35,350 --> 00:10:41,630
It could be poor penetration, weak differentiation, lack of innovation, distribution gaps, or

146
00:10:41,630 --> 00:10:43,350
communication fatigue.

147
00:10:43,350 --> 00:10:49,910
I'd conduct a brand audit, internal performance analysis, retailer feedback, and fresh consumer

148
00:10:49,910 --> 00:10:50,910
insight.

149
00:10:50,910 --> 00:10:55,990
Based on that, I'd refresh the communication angle, refine the positioning, or introduce

150
00:10:55,990 --> 00:10:57,830
new pack formats.

151
00:10:57,830 --> 00:11:02,870
usually comes from unlocking a new consumer segment or strengthening the brand's role

152
00:11:02,870 --> 00:11:04,630
in an existing segment.

153
00:11:04,630 --> 00:11:05,630
Good.

154
00:11:05,630 --> 00:11:10,950
Now, suppose after your audit you discover that younger consumers simply don't connect

155
00:11:10,950 --> 00:11:12,510
with your brand.

156
00:11:12,510 --> 00:11:14,630
What steps would you take?

157
00:11:14,630 --> 00:11:19,350
Younger consumers value authenticity, convenience, and simplicity.

158
00:11:19,350 --> 00:11:23,870
I'd update the brand tone, more energetic, genuine, and relatable.

159
00:11:23,870 --> 00:11:30,770
I'd strengthen digital first communication and maybe collaborate with influencers who align with the brand's character.

160
00:11:30,770 --> 00:11:36,070
I'd also check whether the product features, design, and pricing match their lifestyle.

161
00:11:36,070 --> 00:11:40,370
If necessary, I'd introduce a variant tailored to younger needs.

162
00:11:40,370 --> 00:11:42,570
Let's talk about innovation.

163
00:11:42,570 --> 00:11:47,070
How do you decide if a new product is worth adding to the portfolio?

164
00:11:47,070 --> 00:11:53,370
First, I identify whether the product solves a real problem or creates a new occasion for usage.

165
00:11:53,370 --> 00:11:58,810
Then, I validate the consumer need, estimate potential demand, and study whether the product

166
00:11:58,810 --> 00:12:01,890
enhances the brand's overall value.

167
00:12:01,890 --> 00:12:07,450
Financial viability is critical, margins, production feasibility, and scalability.

168
00:12:07,450 --> 00:12:13,490
A strong innovation idea must be relevant, profitable, and aligned with brand strategy.

169
00:12:13,490 --> 00:12:14,490
Fantastic.

170
00:12:14,490 --> 00:12:20,650
Now, imagine the company wants to launch a premium extension for a mass-market brand.

171
00:12:20,650 --> 00:12:23,790
Some people argue it'll confuse consumers.

172
00:12:23,790 --> 00:12:26,150
What would be your viewpoint?

173
00:12:26,150 --> 00:12:31,210
Premium extensions can work if the value is clearly communicated and the core brand promise

174
00:12:31,210 --> 00:12:32,710
remains untouched.

175
00:12:32,710 --> 00:12:38,550
I'd ensure the premium variant has distinct features, superior quality, unique ingredients,

176
00:12:38,550 --> 00:12:40,690
or a differentiated experience.

177
00:12:40,690 --> 00:12:42,810
The key is maintaining clarity.

178
00:12:42,810 --> 00:12:47,650
The mass variant stays accessible while the premium variant offers an upgrade.

179
00:12:47,650 --> 00:12:52,650
If executed well, it can bring new customers and increase overall brand value.

180
00:12:52,650 --> 00:12:54,370
Very good.

181
00:12:54,370 --> 00:12:56,370
Moving to communication.

182
00:12:56,370 --> 00:13:01,570
Let's say your agency presents a campaign that is extremely creative but doesn't clearly

183
00:13:01,570 --> 00:13:02,570
communicate.

184
00:13:02,570 --> 00:13:03,570
Good morning.

185
00:13:03,570 --> 00:13:05,610
Thank you for joining us today.

186
00:13:05,610 --> 00:13:08,130
Let's begin with a quick introduction.

187
00:13:08,130 --> 00:13:12,010
Tell me about yourself and your branding experience.

188
00:13:12,010 --> 00:13:13,010
Good morning.

189
00:13:13,010 --> 00:13:14,290
Thank you for having me.

190
00:13:14,290 --> 00:13:20,210
I'm a marketing professional with experience in brand strategy, consumer insights, trade

191
00:13:20,210 --> 00:13:22,930
marketing and new product development.

192
00:13:22,930 --> 00:13:29,490
I've worked in FMCG categories where I handled brand campaigns, market analysis and full

193
00:13:29,490 --> 00:13:31,090
year brand planning.

194
00:13:31,090 --> 00:13:38,010
I enjoy combining creative ideas with data-driven decisions to build strong, meaningful brands.

195
00:13:38,010 --> 00:13:39,090
Great to hear.

196
00:13:39,090 --> 00:13:43,130
Tell me how you usually assess the current performance of a brand.

197
00:13:43,130 --> 00:13:45,930
I analyze four key areas.

198
00:13:45,930 --> 00:13:50,210
Market data, consumer behavior, competitor activity,

199
00:13:50,210 --> 00:13:52,370
and internal performance.

200
00:13:52,370 --> 00:13:56,610
I look at penetration, frequency, distribution gaps,

201
00:13:56,610 --> 00:13:59,890
region-wise sales, and channel performance.

202
00:13:59,890 --> 00:14:02,650
Then I evaluate consumer perception,

203
00:14:02,650 --> 00:14:05,950
brand equity scores, and digital sentiment.

204
00:14:05,950 --> 00:14:10,390
I also track competitor pricing, innovation, and promotions.

205
00:14:10,390 --> 00:14:12,650
This helps me identify whether the challenge

206
00:14:12,650 --> 00:14:16,890
is awareness, availability, affordability, or relevance.

207
00:14:16,890 --> 00:14:19,650
And then I shape strategies accordingly.

208
00:14:19,650 --> 00:14:22,970
Brand managers often balance long-term plans

209
00:14:22,970 --> 00:14:25,230
with short-term sales pressure.

210
00:14:25,230 --> 00:14:26,850
How do you manage that?

211
00:14:26,850 --> 00:14:29,610
I said clear KPIs for each.

212
00:14:29,610 --> 00:14:32,850
Long-term KPIs include brand equity,

213
00:14:32,850 --> 00:14:35,290
penetration, product roadmap,

214
00:14:35,290 --> 00:14:37,370
and strengthening positioning.

215
00:14:37,370 --> 00:14:40,990
Short-term KPIs include quarterly sales numbers,

216
00:14:40,990 --> 00:14:45,550
trade activation, availability, and tactical promotions.

217
00:14:45,550 --> 00:14:51,070
I make sure that even short-term promotions reflect the brand's strategic direction.

218
00:14:51,070 --> 00:14:55,710
Tactical moves should support the long-term narrative, not dilute it.

219
00:14:55,710 --> 00:15:00,310
Tell me about a situation where you helped a struggling brand recover.

220
00:15:00,310 --> 00:15:06,830
I once handled a variant that was losing share due to both taste issues and weak communication.

221
00:15:06,830 --> 00:15:11,310
I ran retail audits, consumer groups, and blind taste tests.

222
00:15:11,310 --> 00:15:14,670
The insight showed the product needed a better formulation

223
00:15:14,670 --> 00:15:17,230
and clearer value communication.

224
00:15:17,230 --> 00:15:20,270
We improved the recipe, upgraded the packaging,

225
00:15:20,270 --> 00:15:22,710
and launched a digital first campaign

226
00:15:22,710 --> 00:15:25,270
focusing on taste superiority.

227
00:15:25,270 --> 00:15:28,390
Within six months, repeat purchases improved,

228
00:15:28,390 --> 00:15:29,830
distribution picked up,

229
00:15:29,830 --> 00:15:32,510
and the brand regained lost share.

230
00:15:32,510 --> 00:15:36,550
How do you decide when it's time to refresh packaging?

231
00:15:36,550 --> 00:15:41,430
I look at consumer relevance, shelf standout, and functionality.

232
00:15:41,430 --> 00:15:47,030
If consumers say the pack looks outdated, if it blends into the shelf too easily, or if

233
00:15:47,030 --> 00:15:50,550
there are usability issues, it's time to refresh.

234
00:15:50,550 --> 00:15:56,310
But I ensure recognizability stays intact, so loyal customers don't get confused.

235
00:15:56,310 --> 00:16:00,310
A refresh should improve identity, not disrupt it.

236
00:16:00,310 --> 00:16:03,150
Cross-functional collaboration is important.

237
00:16:03,150 --> 00:16:04,550
How do you handle that?

238
00:16:04,550 --> 00:16:10,970
I work closely with R&D, supply chain, sales, finance, and procurement.

239
00:16:10,970 --> 00:16:17,230
I run regular check-ins, share clear briefs, and align everyone toward the brand objective.

240
00:16:17,230 --> 00:16:20,730
I believe transparency and clarity are essential.

241
00:16:20,730 --> 00:16:25,670
When every team understands the why, execution becomes much smoother.

242
00:16:25,670 --> 00:16:28,450
How do you typically brief an agency?

243
00:16:28,450 --> 00:16:32,890
I structure briefs around the brand challenge, target in the...

244
00:16:32,890 --> 00:16:36,070
Good morning and thank you for joining us today.

245
00:16:36,070 --> 00:16:42,550
Could you start by telling me about your professional background and why you chose brand management?

246
00:16:42,550 --> 00:16:43,550
